Similar analysis could be done for each role.  For some
roles (ranger, wizard), the results are obvious, and do
favor ranged attacks.  For others (valkyrie, samari), I find
that the results favor mel=E9e.  (Knight is a very special
case: mel=E9e until the quest, magic missle like crazy after.)

> There's rarely such a thing possible in practise as ranged-only attacks.
> So promoting "avoid melee combat as long as you can do ranged damage" is
> indeed the *much better* rule of thumb that the opposite (that seems at
> best be only valid in your personal monk approach).

I'm sceptical about promoting a rule of thumb at all.  I'd
say that the rule of thumb would be to use whichever mode of
fighting which leaves you in the best shape afterwards.
Where best shape means capable of ranged attack if
necessary: never, never use ranged attack to the point of
not having any more ranged attacks available (no more manna,
no more daggers, etc.), for example.  (The same thing
obviously holds for mel=E9e: if you're low on hit points,
don't mel=E9e, or even let the monster get close.)

Also consider not fighting at all.  With a ring of combat
and enough wands of teleport (and the game usually gives you
a lot), you rarely have to choose between mel=E9e and ranged
anyway.  Again, consider the cost.  You want to be sure
of having plenty of teleport available when you absolutely
don't want to fight.

Another rule is play with what the RGN gives you.  I
recently had no end of fun with a ranseur and a ring of
invisibility.  When a ranger stumbles over a lot of arrow
traps early on, or a Valk gets Grayswandir with an early
sacrifice, it's obvious what to do, but I've also played a
Monk with GDSM and Grayswandir.  (Got Grayswandir first
sacrifice, and stumbled on blessed +5 GDSM in an early
bones.  Then kicked like crazy until the experience level
was high enough that the -20 to chance to hit didn't
matter.)

